// TEST: Bounded Let Min Violated (aborts)
// ============================================================================
// The MIN-SIDE twin of struct-aborts/012, and it is not symmetry for its own
// sake — it closes a hole that 012 and basic/035 cannot close between them.
//
// `Ast.boundedConjuncts` builds the two endpoint checks in SEPARATE branches:
//   min  ->  OpLe (lo, subject)      -- lo <= x
//   max  ->  OpLe (subject, hi)      -- x <= hi
// The operands are in opposite positions, so the branches fail independently.
// Now consider the min branch built backwards, as `OpLe (subject, lo)`:
//
//   * basic/035 still passes. Every bounded binding there sits exactly ON an
//     endpoint — which is the right way to prove INCLUSIVITY, and is exactly
//     what makes it blind here, because `lo <= lo` and `lo >= lo` are both
//     true. An endpoint value cannot distinguish a comparison from its
//     converse.
//   * struct-aborts/012 still passes. It violates `max`, and the max branch
//     is untouched.
//
// So a reversed min comparison — a live check that can never fire — survives
// the whole suite. This file is the witness that does distinguish them: -1 is
// strictly BELOW the minimum, where `-1 <= 0` and `0 <= -1` finally disagree.
//
// The `(min)` label is the second thing pinned. `synthesizeBoundChecks`
// recovers the side labels by a POSITIONAL ZIP against a second traversal of
// the same type node, so labels and conjuncts are two parallel lists that
// must stay in the same order. Its length-mismatch fallback (label "bound")
// catches a DROPPED endpoint, but a SWAPPED pair keeps both lengths equal and
// would slip through silently — reported as the wrong endpoint, which is
// worse than no label at all, because it sends the reader to the wrong end of
// their own declaration. Pinning `(min)` here and `(max)` in 012 is what makes
// the pair unswappable.
//
// `max=3` is untouched and satisfied; only the lower bound is violated, so
// the abort can come from nowhere else.
// ABORT: Bound violation in 'x' (min)
let x: Int<min=0, max=3> = -1
let y = x + 1
